<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Simple cutaneous abscess of the right forearm (L02.413)</content>. A small abscess after a splinter, without fever, spreading cellulitis or systemic signs. Under the IDSA guideline incision and drainage is the treatment, and antibiotics add little for a simple abscess in a well person. He is afebrile, so a malaria test is not needed today; a fever would change that. [S]<list><item>Incision and drainage under local anesthesia today, with a wound culture; no retained splinter found.</item><item>Dressing changes daily; warm soaks.</item><item>No antibiotic unless the culture or the course changes the picture.</item><item>Return for fever, spreading redness, or red streaks; a fever means a malaria smear the same day as well.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Relapsing P. vivax malaria, after radical cure (B51.9)</content>. No fever since treatment. [S]<list><item>Continue the fever action plan.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Practice guidelines for the diagnosis and management of skin and soft tissue infections: 2014 update by the Infectious Diseases Society of America (Stevens DL, et al. Clin Infect Dis 2014;59(2):e10-e52) (2014)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Nurse wound check in two days. Culture result by telephone. Next scheduled visit 2026-07-28: pre-travel visit: yellow fever, hepatitis A, hepatitis B and typhoid vaccines, and the travel plan. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
